#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179454 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/id #uuid "734d74ca-26cf-57d3-b3dc-fec66d7e0264" :question/math-academy-id "e-6286" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/001487e4a94988a24d15702a18692e9deb2495a7cc7ec5c9f71b98fea13e0ce8.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $O(0,0),$ and the path $C_2$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = -4,$ evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $\\mathbf{F}(x, y) = (2x-3y) \\,\\mathbf{i} + (2y-5x)\\,\\mathbf {j}$ and $C = C_1\\cup C_2.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}\n+\n \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r}.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path $C_2$ is the line segment from $O$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(c-b) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0 \\lt t \\lt 1.$ Along this segment, we have $x = t$ and $y = t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(2y-5x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3t)\\,i+(2t-5t)\\,j \\\\  & =-t\\,i-3t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(-t\\,i-3t\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-t\\cdot 1+(-3t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4+[-2{t}^{2}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-4+[-2+0] \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "2e6103cc-7be6-5eb6-ab5a-aa5aff3fa423" :question/math-academy-id "e-6319" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/38ab4af726729b5c9804f4aa13801d637e5e62eda085d6611174586827ef8c11.png)\n\nConsider the integral $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{r},$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $FGHF$ shown above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction. Express this integral as a sum of line integrals along $C_1, C_2,$ and $C_3.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC = \\{-C_1\\} \\cup \\{-C_2\\} \\cup C_3,\n$$\n\nwhere $-C_1$ and $-C_2$ are the same paths as $C_1$ and $C_2$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179530 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/id #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9535" :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = (3x+2) \\: \\mathbf{i} - y^3 \\: \\mathbf{j}$ and let $C$ be a path in $\\mathbb R^2.$ Write $\\displaystyle \\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}$ in the form $\\displaystyle\\int_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x +  Q(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F}(x,y) = P(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{i} + Q(x,y) \\: \\mathbf{j}\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y) \\: \\text{d}x + Q(x,y) \\: \\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P = 3x+2$ and $Q = -y^3.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(3x+2)\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf979321-309f-5a72-b8f1-6b36a93d2b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2}+5)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338822} {:answer/id #uuid "668691db-ecd9-5882-94a7-aeb545c4c159" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338823} {:answer/id #uuid "dddbbf8e-7d48-5cf3-a8d0-decf034ccf40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338824} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb55056-3bd1-5109-abe5-0a658fc3ae4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338825} {:answer/id #uuid "31d19b95-7e43-5a93-b108-3447de178d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31d19b95-7e43-5a93-b108-3447de178d8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338826} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffa5c498-2566-5a14-bbf7-d5603ec0574e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "287c4c83-1be4-5535-a78c-1deaf55cde76" :question/math-academy-id "q-125370" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)={y}^{2}\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j+5\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y+5\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338827 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3715e9e-3683-5bc0-b82e-81ccbf1603b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\text{d}x+({y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338829} {:answer/id #uuid "e8cd4484-0b0d-5ae4-abe2-6dac077cc733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(x-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338830} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c52da8-959c-567e-bdf7-9b115d6ac413" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}({y}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338831} {:answer/id #uuid "514f012e-c2a0-5c77-bad7-0b7e35e869a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338832} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7a1a33-96e7-5fd4-a228-7f7dfbd49520" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}2\\,\\text{d}x+(x-3y)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338833}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8cd4484-0b0d-5ae4-abe2-6dac077cc733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(x-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338830} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb89f632-48d7-5ccf-b3b5-776a801a1524" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdf120e1-15c5-5edc-882e-a7b8e239c73c" :question/math-academy-id "q-125416" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)=(x-3y)\\,i+2\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{t}^{2}+1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}(x-3y)\\,\\text{d}x+2\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338834 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9582b85e-fc4e-5578-a500-ab8e88447f5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}x\\,\\text{d}x+(3y-1)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338836} {:answer/id #uuid "0dbfebf8-bd10-50e3-a9e0-5dd9a60adc7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}x-4\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338837} {:answer/id #uuid "a846a046-5495-534d-82c3-a39b0377b1a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(3x-1)\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338838} {:answer/id #uuid "a288affd-b59a-5731-b5b1-579f7a8652fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}4\\,\\text{d}x-(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338839} {:answer/id #uuid "6a99e9d3-e89d-5a2a-84a7-a5eca44a552e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}(3y-1)\\,\\text{d}x+x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a288affd-b59a-5731-b5b1-579f7a8652fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}4\\,\\text{d}x-(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338839} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ec17be-75c3-534c-8c33-75133f074c4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338835}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40b96580-71b1-5acf-9cab-b28199e5d04e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125417" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y)=4\\,i-(2y-3x)\\,j$ and let $C$ be the path defined by $r(t)=⟨t,\\,3t-1⟩$ for $t\\in [0,1].$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}4\\,\\text{d}x-(2y-3x)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a13512f4-c7e7-5eea-98fb-495003126171" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∇({x}^{2}-y+2z)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338843} {:answer/id #uuid "343eec27-eaa8-5e5b-8259-9d7dfdcdc4f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}∇({x}^{2}-y+2z)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338844} {:answer/id #uuid "185bda7f-d40b-5456-b569-9b9de1d4c609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,i-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338845} {:answer/id #uuid "b75ccf0b-5123-5716-95b7-50327cc93c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j+2z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338846} {:answer/id #uuid "a5b55868-4bb8-5a71-a524-1a7a4228cde7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i-j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b75ccf0b-5123-5716-95b7-50327cc93c80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j+2z\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338846} :answer-field/id #uuid "fecbb061-bfa5-53d8-ad78-33c0c65a1adc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "58a9e20b-0a6a-52a8-ac51-bb7071b3781e" :question/math-academy-id "q-125423" :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y+2z\\,\\text{d}z$ is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P={x}^{2},Q=-y,R=2z.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j+2z\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-y\\,\\text{d}y+2z\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F={x}^{2}\\,i-y\\,j+2z\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f19e12e-b6cb-5fa5-80c2-99666f56df00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{4}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338850} {:answer/id #uuid "b8bedc6f-bd56-595e-a582-406e2975212c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x\\,i+j+3{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338851} {:answer/id #uuid "d5578898-b3db-5fd9-bf0e-d0076a31a27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\,i+y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338852} {:answer/id #uuid "f9c97414-4f9d-55ea-a941-7b65a9e573ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∇({x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338853} {:answer/id #uuid "d8e8ca17-959f-557f-b54f-78dea51661ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∇(2x+3{z}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5578898-b3db-5fd9-bf0e-d0076a31a27a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\,i+y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338852} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0c04d3-3405-5b71-b03a-710772836709" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a8e91f33-d259-5586-a72c-0ad1065dc0eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-125427" :question/problem "If ${\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z$ is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P={x}^{2},Q=y,R={z}^{3}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i+y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F={x}^{2}\\,i+y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8926a693-1f7e-567d-a227-1b4fa9e92923" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178530} {:answer/id #uuid "d24f1213-97fc-58c4-a966-fccb5d6dcdec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178531} {:answer/id #uuid "0613f2d8-f1ae-5a9d-ba57-9fc9262ff1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178532} {:answer/id #uuid "31292df5-b34d-5769-a65d-5343211b9c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178533} {:answer/id #uuid "60387dd7-0cc8-5601-a6e6-9e5939d3c07d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31292df5-b34d-5769-a65d-5343211b9c67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178533} :answer-field/id #uuid "23ee968d-e67d-5109-b10b-5cc8ac6187ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178529}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eca1e45a-1d3f-5ae4-a5ee-83a793b7f6b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-39342" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨2,0⟩+t(⟨1,1⟩-⟨2,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨2-t,t⟩ \\\\  & =(2-t)\\,i+t\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=2-t$ and $y=t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(x+y)\\,i+(x-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2-t+t)\\,i+(2-t-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =(2\\,i+(2-2t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i+j) \\\\  & =-2+(2-2t) \\\\  & =-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2-{t}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2-(1-0) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bbe46702-0779-5802-ba7f-2b20370f0a6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338857} {:answer/id #uuid "2083c2e6-7d2a-5e0f-8192-a12055690202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338858} {:answer/id #uuid "5e8c4b83-ba8a-5d16-bd8c-e5ed99be2ad3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338859} {:answer/id #uuid "a3b02fcc-a142-5e8b-a69a-0fd4ab1bf686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338860} {:answer/id #uuid "2224fb9e-af87-566e-a8ea-6a6ad44e203e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3b02fcc-a142-5e8b-a69a-0fd4ab1bf686" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338860} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3ea1cae-7b0b-5f81-8c1f-98fa88663273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff3b2637-63d0-5a51-b3a4-46d0fad2a2ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-39673" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+t(⟨1,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,1+t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+(1+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3(1+t))\\,i+(t+(1+t))\\,j \\\\  & =(-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =((-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-3-t+2t+1 \\\\  & =t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-2t]}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-2-0]+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d784816-14e3-5c0d-be28-8d2e041f355a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178542} {:answer/id #uuid "c1da1434-cc23-5789-a91f-6a2625bc25cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178543} {:answer/id #uuid "04e4bb00-e83e-5185-915e-cda7617c7335" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178544} {:answer/id #uuid "438d5d79-fb9c-5c0a-9f6d-01201cea0e5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178545} {:answer/id #uuid "70a7766d-d168-54e8-9ee8-b8df185dee69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70a7766d-d168-54e8-9ee8-b8df185dee69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178546} :answer-field/id #uuid "fde3ad9e-b9ef-522c-a7a2-d1a50bb43f20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e395277-17c1-5003-9ac0-10817fce6c94" :question/math-academy-id "q-39674" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/512905447203d1d3a96781d20d586f9c4cf5cc9392fab50607b6fc77e9b00121.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,4),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,4)$ to the point $B(0,4).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+y\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\n${C}_{1}$ is the section of parabola $y={x}^{2}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<2.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+y\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =t+2{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(t+2{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-2 \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{t}^{4}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}-2 \\\\  & =[2+8]-2 \\\\  & =10-2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a41807ba-6bc5-5c5a-9f9d-cea9dffbd0f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338863} {:answer/id #uuid "26d1c68f-bba9-56a1-923f-b7661f8b0866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338864} {:answer/id #uuid "cc74bfda-7ca7-5d01-8371-6395e6252504" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338865} {:answer/id #uuid "11862e00-139b-546a-9a83-818cdee3e97b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338866} {:answer/id #uuid "66c3ece1-5fdf-5b7c-8b15-e35225726472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c3ece1-5fdf-5b7c-8b15-e35225726472" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338867} :answer-field/id #uuid "c83a343d-c250-5272-8bb1-febadff806e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9aa3be4f-70c9-57d5-8b81-21b143de9479" :question/math-academy-id "q-76473" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14060128/assets/103c586778cdd8181fdc8396a92472df0525fc8e1c77fde9a7c3f731dca6df07.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $OABO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup {-{C}_{2}}\\cup {C}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ is the same path as ${C}_{2}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338868 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:ans